 "id": "2026-11-18-kerch-bridge-span-down",
 "lang": "en",
 "script": [
  {
   "id": "hook",
   "text": "Part of Russia's bridge to Crimea is in the sea. Why does it matter?"
  },
  {
   "id": "f1",
   "text": "Ukraine struck the Kerch Bridge two nights in a row. Footage geolocated by CNN and NBC News shows a road span collapsed into the water. Ukraine's security service says a steel arch was destroyed and the rail section damaged. Zelensky called it unprecedented."
  },
  {
   "id": "f2",
   "text": "The bridge is about nineteen kilometres long, the main land link from Russia to occupied Crimea, and a key supply route for Russian forces. Russia halted all traffic. Vedomosti reports one rail track has reopened. Moscow opened a terrorism case."
  },
  {
   "id": "f3",
   "text": "Kyiv was hit too. Officials say missiles and drones struck its North and South bridges, killing at least two. Russia claims it downed one thousand four hundred forty-one drones overnight, a record. On Thursday, Putin meets Trump and Xi."
  },
  {
   "id": "cta",
   "text": "Will the Kerch Bridge fully reopen before the new year? Yes or no?"
  }
 ],
